00:02:13.000 --> 00:03:04.000
On the night of Sunday, September 5, 1982, the crew aboard a fishing vessel called The Decade threw a party. They were celebrating the coming end of the fishing season. While they drank inside their boat, a storm raged across the Southern Alaskan coast outside. At around six the following morning, a hungover crew member stuck it out to the deck to be sick over the side. The sun was breaking over the misty horizon as rain drizzled across the cold blue waters of the Pacific. The crew member took in his surroundings and caught sight of something. Another fishing vessel, a 58-foot purse sena dubbed to the investor, was slowly drifting out to sea.
1982年9月5日星期日晚上，一艘名为“十年号”（The Decade）的渔船上的船员们举办了一场派对。他们正在庆祝捕鱼季即将结束。当他们在船舱内喝酒时，船外的阿拉斯加南部海岸正狂风暴雨。次日清晨六点左右，一名宿醉的船员来到甲板上，在船舷边呕吐。太阳正从雾蒙蒙的地平线上升起，细雨飘落在太平洋冰冷的蓝色水面上。这名船员环顾四周，突然看到了什么。另一艘渔船，一艘名为“投资者号”（The Investor）的58英尺围网渔船，正缓慢地向海上漂去。

5
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Barely visible in the gloomy weather was the investor's pilot house, where its navigation equipment was held. At the helm was a man. He looked to be in his 20s and was of average height with a stocky build. He was wearing a baseball cap, though some of his light-colored hair was visible. The final phase of the salmon season was being reopened by Alaska's Department of Fish and Game that day. It seemed the investor was getting a head start. The man behind the wheel was assumed to be the investor's captain, 28-year-old Mark Colterst. The decade crew member waved to him. The man raised a single hand in response
在阴沉的天气里，几乎看不清“投资者号”的驾驶室，那里存放着导航设备。掌舵的是一名男子。他看起来20多岁，中等身材，体格健壮。他戴着一顶棒球帽，但仍能看到一些浅色的头发。阿拉斯加州渔猎部当天重新开放了鲑鱼捕捞季的最后阶段。看起来“投资者号”是想抢占先机。掌舵的男子被认为是“投资者号”的船长，28岁的马克·科尔瑟斯特（Mark Colthurst）。“十年号”的船员向他挥手。那名男子举起一只手作为回应，

6
00:03:54.000 --> 00:05:05.000
as he steered the investor silently away. Throughout mid to late 1982, dozens of fishing trawlers were docked in the remote Alaskan village of Craig. Located on Prince of Wales Island in the southern part of the state, Craig faces outwards to the Gulf of Alaska, with several other smaller, largely deserted islands inside. The local community of just 600 people relied on its strong fishing industry, supported by the abundance of halibut and salmon in the surrounding waters. Craig was a stopping point for fishing vessels as they journeyed between Alaska and the continental United States. Boats docked at the village overnight, then set sail shortly before
随后默默地驾驶着“投资者号”离开了。1982年中后期，数十艘拖网渔船停靠在阿拉斯加偏远的克雷格（Craig）村。克雷格位于该州南部的威尔士亲王岛上，面向阿拉斯加湾，岛内还有其他几个较小的、基本荒无人烟的岛屿。当地仅有的600名居民依靠强大的渔业为生，周围水域丰富的比目鱼和鲑鱼为他们提供了支持。克雷格是渔船往返于阿拉斯加和美国大陆之间的中转站。船只在村里过夜停靠，然后在临近

7
00:05:05.000 --> 00:06:01.000
first delight in search of a fishery, areas of sea where fish are caught in large quantities. Captains and their crews were known to anchor at a fishery for several days before returning to Craig to sell their catch. The fish were then sent to the island's cold storage plant and cannery. The investor was one of the more impressive boats docked in Craig. The state of the art red and grey fiberglass sena was the largest size of boat permitted to fish in Alaskan waters. Valued at around $1.5 million, the vessel was comfortable to live in and decked out with the latest fishing gear. The investor was just one year old when its current captain, Mark Colterst, boarded. Mark was a naturally gifted fisherman from the state of Washington.
黎明时分启航，寻找渔场，即大量捕鱼的海域。船长和船员们通常会在渔场抛锚几天，然后再回到克雷格出售渔获。鱼随后被送到岛上的冷藏厂和罐头厂。在克雷格停靠的船只中，“投资者号”是比较引人注目的一艘。这艘最先进的红灰色玻璃钢围网渔船是阿拉斯加水域允许捕鱼的最大尺寸船只。该船价值约150万美元，居住舒适，并配备了最新的捕鱼设备。当现任船长马克·科尔瑟斯特登船时，“投资者号”才一岁。马克是一位来自华盛顿州的天赋异禀的渔民。

8
00:06:01.000 --> 00:07:04.000
He had taken up fishing in his teens, starting out with a wooden skiff he used to net herring for profit. He then upgraded to an old rusted trawler. During a week-long expedition in 1979, Mark hauled in an impressive $105,000 worth of fish. Eventually, he bought the boat of his dreams, the investor. In a feature article for Pacific Fisherman magazine, Mark explained that he had named the investor after the money he was certain it would make. He hoped it would facilitate his plans to retire at the age of 50. In 1982, Mark arrived in Craig on Sunday, September 5, after two weeks at sea. It was a rare stop. He didn't usually visit the island village. It was also the first time he'd been there in his new boat. A broom was attached to
他十几岁时就开始捕鱼，最初用一艘木制小艇捕捞鲱鱼获利。后来他升级到了一艘生锈的旧拖网渔船。在1979年为期一周的远征中，马克捕获了价值105,000美元的鱼，成绩斐然。最终，他买下了梦想中的船——“投资者号”。在《太平洋渔民》杂志的一篇专题文章中，马克解释说，他以这艘船肯定能赚到的钱为它命名。他希望这能帮助他实现50岁退休的计划。1982年9月5日星期日，马克在海上航行两周后抵达克雷格。这是一个罕见的停靠点。他通常不会访问这个岛村。这也是他第一次驾驶新船来到这里。一根扫帚被系在

9
00:07:04.000 --> 00:08:03.000
the investor's mast, a message to other vessels that it had so far caught 100,000 pounds of salmon. Because there weren't enough moorage spots on Craig's dock to accommodate so many boats, the investor was tethered to the Decade, which was tied to another boat called the Defiant. In the early hours of Monday, September 6, the investor was on the move. Aside from the hungover crew member aboard the Decade, a number of other fishermen noticed the investor quietly slipping away. Mike Colterst had left the investor's tie lines, the heavy braided rope that had connected it to the Decade, on the other boat's deck. Tie lines cost over $100. They were not the type of thing fishermen discarded lightly.
“投资者号”的桅杆上，向其他船只传达它目前已捕获10万磅鲑鱼的信息。由于克雷格码头的泊位不足以容纳这么多船只，“投资者号”被系在“十年号”上，而“十年号”又系在另一艘名为“挑衅号”（The Defiant）的船上。9月6日星期一凌晨，“投资者号”开始移动。除了“十年号”上那名宿醉的船员外，还有其他几名渔民注意到“投资者号”悄悄溜走了。迈克·科尔瑟斯特将“投资者号”的系泊缆绳——连接它与“十年号”的粗编织绳——留在了另一艘船的甲板上。系泊缆绳价值超过100美元。这不是渔民会轻易丢弃的东西。

10
00:08:03.000 --> 00:09:00.000
At around 7.30am, it stopped at Fish Air Island, a small landmass about a mile away. Then a heavy fog rolled in, obscuring the investor from sight. The fog hung low across the harbour for the rest of the day. It finally cleared by the following morning of Tuesday, September 7, revealing that the investor hadn't moved from its spot alongside Fish Air Island. Just after 4pm that day, fishermen Bruce Anderson noticed thick black smoke by Fish Air Island. He wondered whether it was engine exhaust from a vessel that had just started its motor. The smoke remained heavy and didn't dissipate. Bruce grew concerned and grabbed a pair of binoculars. He recognised the investor.
早上7点30分左右，它在约一英里外的一个小陆块——鱼空气岛（Fish Egg Island）停了下来。随后大雾弥漫，遮住了“投资者号”的身影。大雾在港口上空笼罩了整整一天。直到次日9月7日星期二清晨，雾气终于散去，显示“投资者号”并没有离开鱼空气岛旁的位置。当天下午4点刚过，渔民布鲁斯·安德森（Bruce Anderson）注意到鱼空气岛附近有浓浓的黑烟。他怀疑那是刚启动引擎的船只排出的废气。烟雾依然浓重，没有消散。布鲁斯感到担忧，拿起双筒望远镜。他认出了“投资者号”。

11
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:55.000
It was engulfed in flames. Bruce and his wife, Jan Kittleson, raced towards the fire in their own boat. As they drew closer, they saw a smaller boat speeding towards them from the investor's direction. It was a 20ft red and grey fiberglass skiff, a craft used to manoeuvre a purse seen as fishing net into place before the net is pulled on board. Bruce and Jan identified the skiff as belonging to the investor, as it was made of the same fiberglass and painted to match the larger vessel. But they didn't recognise the lone man in it. He had tanned skin and slightly long hair, partially obscured by a baseball cap. Bruce thought he had a California surfer look about him.
它被火焰吞没了。布鲁斯和他的妻子简·基特尔森（Jan Kittleson）驾驶自己的船冲向火场。当他们靠近时，看到一艘较小的船正从“投资者号”的方向向他们疾驰而来。那是一艘20英尺长的红灰色玻璃钢小艇，这种船用于在围网被拉上船之前，将围网引导到位。布鲁斯和简认出这艘小艇属于“投资者号”，因为它是由同样的玻璃钢制成，且漆色与大船相匹配。但他们不认识艇上那名孤身一人的男子。他皮肤黝黑，头发稍长，被一顶棒球帽遮住了一部分。布鲁斯觉得他看起来像个加州冲浪者。

12
00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:37.000
Jan waved for the man to stop, but he seemed determined to avoid them. Bruce cut the skiff off, forcing the man to stop about 15ft away. Are there people on the boat? Jan shouted. The man didn't reply. Jan repeated herself several times. Wide-eyed, the man finally said, Yes, there are people on the boat. He then sped off at full throttle towards Craig. Sue Dominovsky and her boyfriend Paul Page were watching the chaos unfolding from the docks in Craig.
简挥手示意那名男子停下，但他似乎执意要避开他们。布鲁斯截住了小艇，迫使那名男子在约15英尺外停下。“船上有人吗？”简大喊道。那名男子没有回答。简重复了几遍。那名男子瞪大眼睛，终于说道：“是的，船上有人。”随后他全速向克雷格方向疾驰而去。苏·多米诺夫斯基（Sue Dominovsky）和她的男友保罗·佩奇（Paul Page）正在克雷格的码头上看着混乱的场面。

13
00:10:37.000 --> 00:11:29.000
It seemed like every boat in town was moving towards the fire, except one. A man in a red and grey fiberglass skiff was heading to shore. He looked about 20 or 21 years old and roughly 150lbs. He had brown or blonde hair, and was wearing a baseball cap with a logo on it. What was striking was how unbothered the man looked, as if nothing out of the ordinary was happening. He was driving at such rapid speed, he caused waves that flooded the engine of a passing rescue party. The man landed the skiff with a crash, hitting its bow into the dock, that Sue Dominovsky was standing on. She felt a shake beneath her feet.
似乎镇上所有的船都在向火场移动，只有一艘例外。一名驾驶红灰色玻璃钢小艇的男子正向岸边驶去。他看起来大约20或21岁，体重约150磅。他留着棕色或金色的头发，戴着一顶印有标志的棒球帽。令人震惊的是，这名男子看起来如此镇定，仿佛什么异常的事情都没有发生。他驾驶的速度极快，激起的波浪淹没了路过救援船只的引擎。那名男子猛地靠岸，船头撞上了苏·多米诺夫斯基所站的码头。她感到脚下一阵震动。

14
00:11:29.000 --> 00:12:10.000
The young man leapt from the skiff, and hurriedly tied it to the dock. His face was pale, but his cheeks appeared slightly flushed. He carried what looked like a shoebox under one arm. Sue and her boyfriend Paul asked if the man had come from Craig's home. He came from the boat that was burning. No, he replied, before explaining that he was in a hurry. He fidgeted awkwardly, and kept his gaze downcast. Sue then asked if there was anyone on the burning boat.
那名年轻人从艇上跳下，匆忙将它系在码头上。他的脸色苍白，但脸颊似乎微微泛红。他的一只胳膊下夹着一个看起来像鞋盒的东西。苏和她的男友保罗问那名男子是否来自克雷格的家。“他来自那艘着火的船。”“不，”他回答道，随后解释说他很赶时间。他局促不安，目光低垂。苏随后问着火的船上是否还有人。

15
00:12:10.000 --> 00:13:07.000
It was hard to get a clear answer. The man spoke slowly, as though in shock. He reiterated that he had to go, yet he just remained standing there. He offered the couple use of his skiff, before saying that he had to go call the Coast Guard. No one's called the Coast Guard yet? Sue cried out in surprise. The man walked away without a reply. The fire was fierce, producing waves of heat so intense it was impossible to move in to stop it. It consumed the investor's wheelhouse and spread along the back deck. A smaller, more nimble vessel circled closer to the investor to see if anyone was on board.
很难得到明确的回答。那名男子说话很慢，仿佛处于震惊之中。他重申他必须走了，但他只是站在那里。他提出让这对夫妇使用他的小艇，然后说他必须去给海岸警卫队打电话。“还没人给海岸警卫队打电话吗？”苏惊讶地喊道。那名男子没有回答就走开了。火势凶猛，产生的热浪极其强烈，根本无法靠近扑灭。它吞噬了“投资者号”的驾驶室，并蔓延到后甲板。一艘更小、更灵活的船只绕着“投资者号”靠近，查看船上是否有人。

16
00:13:07.000 --> 00:14:00.000
Nothing was visible, aside from a few unidentifiable objects bobbing in the water. With no way to get close enough to douse the flames, onlookers watched helplessly. At 4.20pm, Alaska State Trooper Bob Anderson was alerted to the situation. He took his personal boat to Fishag Island, where a small fleet surrounded the investor from a safe distance. By now, the investor's entire cabin, galley, bridge and scene net were ablaze. The speed and viciousness of the fire left Trooper Anderson in no doubt. It had been deliberately lit. Trooper Anderson rushed back to Craig to locate the investor's captain.
除了水面上漂浮着一些无法辨认的物体外，什么也看不见。由于无法靠近灭火，围观者只能无助地看着。下午4点20分，阿拉斯加州警官鲍勃·安德森（Bob Anderson）接到警报。他驾驶自己的船前往鱼空气岛，那里有一小支船队在安全距离外包围了“投资者号”。此时，“投资者号”的整个船舱、厨房、舰桥和围网都在燃烧。火势的速度和凶猛程度让安德森警官毫不怀疑：这是蓄意纵火。安德森警官赶回克雷格寻找“投资者号”的船长。

17
00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:57.000
He was informed that Mark Colterst hadn't been fishing alone that season. He was joined by four deckhands, 18-year-old Chris Heyman and Mike Stewart, Dean Moon and Jerome Cowan, who were all 19. Also travelling aboard the investor were Mark's wife Irene and their two children, 5-year-old Kimberly and 4-year-old John. Irene was 3 months pregnant. Trooper Anderson got to work tracking down everyone who had been on the investor. The last time the Coltersts and their crew had been seen was two days earlier, on Sunday, September 5. That afternoon, Mark and his deckhands returned to Craig to offload salmon they'd caught. Payment wouldn't come through until the following day.
他被告知马克·科尔瑟斯特那个捕鱼季并不是独自一人。随行的还有四名甲板工：18岁的克里斯·海曼（Chris Heyman）和迈克·斯图尔特（Mike Stewart），以及19岁的迪恩·穆恩（Dean Moon）和杰罗姆·考恩（Jerome Cowan）。随“投资者号”旅行的还有马克的妻子艾琳（Irene）和他们的两个孩子：5岁的金伯利（Kimberly）和4岁的约翰（John）。艾琳当时已怀孕3个月。安德森警官开始着手追踪所有在“投资者号”上的人。科尔瑟斯特一家及其船员最后一次露面是在两天前的9月5日星期日。那天下午，马克和他的甲板工回到克雷格卸下捕获的鲑鱼。报酬要到第二天才能到账。

18
00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:49.000
Faced with some spare time, Mark gave his crew the night off. Dean Moon and Jerome Cowan headed into town. Chris Heyman and Mike Stewart weren't seen with them. It was presumed they remained behind on the investor. Mark Colterst opted to spend the night with his wife and children. It was the family's last chance to be together for a while. Irene, Kimberly and John were flying back to Washington the next day to prepare for Kimberly's starting school. September 5 also happened to be Mark's 28th birthday. The Coltersts had a celebratory dinner at a local restaurant before returning to the investor just before 10 o'clock.
由于有空闲时间，马克给船员们放了一晚假。迪恩·穆恩和杰罗姆·考恩进了城。克里斯·海曼和迈克·斯图尔特没有和他们一起出现。人们推测他们留在了“投资者号”上。马克·科尔瑟斯特选择与妻子和孩子们共度夜晚。这是这家人一段时间内最后一次团聚的机会。艾琳、金伯利和约翰第二天就要飞回华盛顿，为金伯利上学做准备。9月5日恰好也是马克的28岁生日。科尔瑟斯特一家在当地一家餐馆吃了庆祝晚餐，然后在10点前回到“投资者号”。

19
00:15:49.000 --> 00:17:00.000
They were witnessed clamouring over the two boats the investor was tethered to, passing a party in full swing along the way. No one from the investor was sighted after that night. At 8pm, State Trooper Bob Anderson received a call that the fire had been extinguished. The once gleaming investor was now a charred and melted wreck. Trooper Anderson boarded the ruined boat. It was slick from water and leaning slightly to the right, making it difficult to walk on. Where the galley door had once stood was what appeared to be the badly burnt carcass of an animal, perhaps a dog lying on its side with its legs sticking out. On closer inspection, Trooper Anderson realised the corpse was actually human.
